// A call that waits for the network or for time suspends only its task, so
// the other tasks of its worker run during the wait (spec/04-semantics.md,
// Suspend Points). Here the task
// `writer` calls writeline() with a string larger than the buffers of a connection,
// while nothing reads the peer. The call has to wait for room. The task
// `ticker` of the same worker runs during the wait, and then the main task reads
// the whole string. An implementation that blocks the worker in writeline() never
// runs `ticker`, and hits the timeout. The probe assumes that the buffers of a
// connection on the local host hold less than 32 MB together; if they hold more,
// the call does not wait and the probe does not show the property.
// The CHECK lines do not bind the bootstrap interpreter. It runs every worker on
// one host thread, so a blocking call blocks every task (spec/README.md,
// Conformance: scheduling is implementation-defined). The probe on the
// NOT-BINDING line checks the bootstrap's own choice in its place.
// STATUS: ok
// TIMEOUT: 10
// NOT-BINDING: bootstrap bootstrap-only/same_worker_blocking_call.lox
// COVERS: suspend-call Socket.writeline
// CHECK: ticker ran
// CHECK: read all: true
// CHECK: writer done
var srv = listen("127.0.0.1", 0);
var out = connect("127.0.0.1", srv.port());
var inp = srv.accept();
var me = task.self();
var big = "x";
for (var i = 0; i < 25; i = i + 1) big = big + big;
var want = len(big) + 1;
fun writer() {
    out.writeline(big);
    out.close();
    task.send(me, "writer done");
}
fun ticker() {
    print "ticker ran";
    task.send(me, "ticked");
}
task.spawn(writer);
task.spawn(ticker);
task.receive();
var total = 0;
while (total < want) {
    var chunk = inp.read_bytes(1048576);
    if (chunk == "") break;
    total = total + len(chunk);
}
print "read all: " + (total == want);
print task.receive();
